    "result_text": "The full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2 tests, 0 failures \u2014 the acceptance suite is the only test module).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the duplicate-telephone rejection by adding a new step to the `POST /api/owners` OfficeFloor pipeline, following the project's function-injection conventions.\n\n**New files:**\n- `rest/escalation/DuplicateTelephoneException.java` \u2014 checked exception thrown on a collision.\n- `rest/escalation/DuplicateTelephoneExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds **409 Conflict** with a `ProblemDetail` body (consistent with the other escalation handlers).\n- `officefloor/escalation/\u2026DuplicateTelephoneException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n- `rest/function/owner/EnsureUniqueTelephone.java` \u2014 the check step.\n\n**Modified:**\n- `officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted `ensureUniqueTelephone` between `normalizeTelephone` and `build`.\n\n**How it works:** `EnsureUniqueTelephone` runs right after `NormalizeOwnerTelephone` (so the request telephone is already the canonical 10-digit form) and before the entity is built/saved. It scans existing owners via `OwnerRepository.findAll()`, normalizing each stored telephone the same way (strip non-digits) before comparing, so a match on any other owner throws `DuplicateTelephoneException` \u2192 409. Placing it before `build`/`save` means an invalid or missing telephone still returns 400 first (order preserved), and distinct telephones still create normally (201).",
